THE PROBLEM
what ever i will be, we have some limited result
(3 + ...1^2) % 10 = 4
(3 + ...2^2) % 10 = 7
(3 + ...3^2) % 10 = 2
(3 + ...4^2) % 10 = 9
(3 + ...5^2) % 10 = 8
(3 + ...6^2) % 10 = 7
(3 + ...7^2) % 10 = 2
(3 + ...8^2) % 10 = 7
(3 + ...9^2) % 10 = 4

so if we have free space in some index like (5)
it will never find!
we can trapped in infinite loop!


SOLUTION
we have some concept that will improve this situation
but he WILL NOT RESOLVE IT TOTALLY"

1.BETTER FUNCTIONS
    we should use some more complex quadratic function to generate better distributed numbers for new index.
    if used simpler function than  (h(k) + i^2) % m
    like just use                  (i^2) % m
    we will have more hole (2,3,7,8)
    (...1^2) % 10 = 1
    (...2^2) % 10 = 4
    (...3^2) % 10 = 9
    (...4^2) % 10 = 6
    (...5^2) % 10 = 5
    (..6^2)  % 10 = 6
    (...7^2) % 10 = 9
    (...8^2) % 10 = 4
    (...9^2) % 10 = 1

better function:
    index = (h(k) + c_1 i + c_2 i^2) % m
    we have some new term (c_1 * i)
    that will improving distribution


2.BETTER ARRAY SIZE
    if we use primary numbers for size of array (3,5,7,11,13,...)
    if we use 11 instead of 10 for our array size,
    we have better result!
    because composite numbers will act similar on divisor numbers (مقسوم علیه)
    and primary numbers have so limited divisor numbers


3.KEEP LOAD FACTOR LOW
  load factor is density of the array
  (number of current items / size of array)
  so if we keep it low, it means we have some free space
  and by using good function(solution 1)
  and good array size (solution 2)
  the probability of happening this problem will so small!

Note: the good load factor is 75%
      it means when 3/4 of array get full
      we should resize the array
      and rehash the items!

